@flexiarg snatch/have-a-temperament
@title Have a Temperament
@audience policy authors
@tone foundational
@style pattern
@why cascades/declared-skeleton
@how snatch/grim-cuts-the-cascade-and-never-widens-it snatch/promote-the-remedy-before-the-exit snatch/play-the-authored-order-first snatch/lead-with-the-exchange-rule snatch/widen-the-cascade-only-on-evidence

! conclusion: Write the policy that builds and orders your cascade as a pattern of its own, with a named temperament, rather than leaving it in the runner.

  + context: **Policy grain.** The operand is the cascade — its membership and
    its precedence field — not game state. Snatch runs two policy layers: the
    cascade organises play (`checks/playout_snatch.clj:150-158` — filter to the
    patterns whose antecedent holds, sort by precedence, take the first THEN
    that returns an action), and something decides what is in the cascade and in
    what order. Today that something is Clojure, not a pattern: `pi-grim`
    (`:139-143`, whose comment at `:137-138` reads *"Hardcoded — the collection
    cannot reach it"*) and the `overrides` map handed to `pattern-policy`
    (`:160`, `:165`).

  + IF:
    You are choosing which patterns a run will play from, or the order in which
    they are consulted.

  + HOWEVER:
    A policy left in the runner is invisible to the library the run claims to be
    playing from: it cannot be read before the run, cited, refused, or set
    beside another one. `p4ng/app-snatch.tex:231` records the order under
    *Limits* as "authored by hand", and the harness's own re-wiring shows the
    choice is not cosmetic — the same eighteen patterns score +3 or −5 against a
    snatcher under G4 depending on nothing but it.

  + THEN:
    State the temperament as a production rule at policy grain: an IF and a
    HOWEVER that read the cascade and the record of the run so far, and a THEN
    that emits a **cascade edit** — promote, drop, admit — and never a move.
    Name it, give it a `@why`, and let a reviewer read it before the run rather
    than infer it from the score afterwards.

  + BECAUSE:
    Precedence is data on the cascade — `P-validated-R5.md:502` (law O4) states
    it that way and already calls the conflict-resolution order *the
    temperament* — and data that no pattern writes is data no one authored. A
    temperament written as a pattern is reviewable by the discipline that
    governs the patterns it orders. A temperament written as a map literal is
    reviewable only by reading the runner, which is a different audience and a
    different act.
    + evidence: the two temperaments the harness has are `patterns-overrides {}`
      (`checks/playout_snatch.clj:160`) and `exchange-first-overrides
      {:exchange-when-both-sides-gain 0}` (`:165`). Both are one-line map
      literals in the policy file; neither appears anywhere in `library/snatch/`,
      and `checks/snatch-cascade.edn` records that the second costs eight points
      against a snatcher under G4.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - The five methods below are the temperaments that exist in code or are
      recorded as findings. A sixth — re-order precedence by what scored on the
      last run — has no instance anywhere yet and is not written here.
    - No runner reads a policy-grain rule today: `pattern-policy` takes
      `overrides` as a Clojure argument (`:150`). Wiring these THENs into that
      argument is unbuilt, and so is the `:policy` grain guard that would keep a
      policy-grain rule from firing in a game situation.
